# /etc/sudoers.d/agent-team-deploy — installed by deploy/service/install.sh (mode 0440).
# The dispatcher (user __USER__) may deploy and roll back registered services without a password; nothing else.
#
# What runs as root: deploy-service.sh itself — the check of its arguments (slug [a-z0-9-], --sha hex), the lock, the
# ownership of the service's directories, the instance env file, systemctl start / stop of agent-team-svc@<slug>-<colour>,
# the nginx upstream file with nginx -t and reload, the state file and the deploy journal. Its settings come from
# /etc/agent-team/services/<slug>.conf, which only root writes (add-service.sh), and sudo's env_reset keeps the
# caller's environment out, so the caller chooses nothing but the slug, the commit and --rollback / --force.
#
# What does not run as root: everything that reads or executes the product repository — git clone / fetch / checkout,
# the copy into the release, INSTALL_CMD (npm ci) and BUILD_CMD (npm run build). The command text of those two steps
# does come from outside root's files: the `scripts` of package.json (postinstall, build) are written in the product
# repository, and the developer role merges into it without a human. deploy-service.sh therefore runs these steps as
# the service user through `runuser` with a clean environment, and stops before any of them when it cannot drop root.
# The server process is that same user (agent-team-svc@.service).
#
# Still true: any process of __USER__ can start a deploy, a rollback or --force of any registered service.
__USER__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/agent-team/bin/deploy-service.sh
#
# service-host.sh (owner 27.11): the same user may add a second address to a registered service, make an alias the main
# address and drop an alias. As root it runs: the check of its arguments (slug [a-z0-9-], host a plain FQDN), the DNS
# check that the host points at this server, certbot --webroot for that host, the files <slug>-alias-<host>.conf and
# <slug>-old-host.conf under /etc/nginx/sites-available with their links, render-vhost.sh for the main vhost, nginx -t
# and a graceful reload, HOST= / OLD_HOST= in the service definition. Its settings come from root's files and sudo's
# env_reset keeps the caller's environment out, so the caller chooses the command, a registered slug and a host name.
# What a process of __USER__ can therefore do without a human: make any name that already points at this server answer
# with a registered service, switch the main address of a service among its aliases (the old one then redirects), and
# stop an alias. It cannot serve other content, read a key or touch another site's vhost.
__USER__ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/agent-team/bin/service-host.sh
